{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_019vnJmZmeWSZGn3A2pqnjAQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what was written for **Q2: Agent vs simple LLM chain?** (`answers/03-002-agent-vs-simple-llm-chain.md`):\n\n**Core distinction captured:** *Who decides the next step?* — developer at write time (chain) vs LLM at runtime based on tool observations (agent).\n\n**Key content:**\n- **Concept:** Fixed pipeline vs dynamic perceive→plan→act→observe loop — one crisp sentence distinguishes them\n- **Mechanism:** Side-by-side architecture diagrams (chain = linear, agent = branching loop with ReAct pattern)\n- **Tradeoff table:** 8 dimensions — control flow, tool calls, iteration, predictability, latency, cost, debuggability, testing — with clear when-to-use guidance\n- **Spectrum callout:** Fixed chain → LangGraph conditional DAG → plan-and-execute → ReAct agent → multi-agent (the DAG middle ground is the most important production nuance)\n- **Pitfalls:** \"agents are more powerful so just use agents\" (wrong), \"agents use tools and chains don't\" (wrong — the tool-calling distinction is a common misconception), treating the binary as absolute\n- **Verbal script:** 3-min spoken walkthrough with the FAQ RAG example (chain) vs double-charge support ticket (agent)\n- **One-liner recall:** Chain = developer controls flow at write time; agent = LLM controls flow at runtime — use chains when execution graph is predictable\n\nNext up: **Q3 — What makes a system truly agentic? What does NOT qualify?**"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":370,"cache_read_input_tokens":68046,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":370,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8152360c-0f44-4fb0-9978-85e4e0caed76","uuid":"32236fe9-d1d6-4717-bef2-a81e85169553"}
